content.text:
Now add an environment map to give metal and wet asphalt real reflections — this is the biggest "Unity graphics" upgrade. Without addons, I can create a cube render target from the scene once, or use PMREMGenerator.fromScene(). THREE has PMREMGenerator in core. I can generate an env map from the scene (sky + buildings) and assign scene.environment. But doing it after the city is built would include buildings; good for reflections. Cost: one-time render of 6 faces with blur. That would make the wet road and cars look much better.

Let me add after the city is built (after buildings, before vehicles?). Actually scene.environment with PMREM includes the sky and buildings. Place after lights and sky but before adding many meshes? PMREM fromScene renders the scene from origin. If done late (after full world), it captures buildings and lamps. It's a one-time cost ~ maybe 100ms.

But scene.environment applies to all MeshStandardMaterials, adding reflections that may wash out the night look. We can set scene.environmentIntensity (r170 supports scene.environmentIntensity? Added in r163 I believe). Yes, Scene.environmentIntensity exists in recent versions.

Let's add it near the end of setup, before the loop:

const pmrem = new THREE.PMREMGenerator(renderer);
const envRT = pmrem.fromScene(scene, 0.04, 0.1, 2000);
scene.environment = envRT.texture;
scene.environmentIntensity = 0.35;

Wait, fromScene renders the scene including the skydome. Should be fine. But it will also include the avatar/cars? Place before vehicles spawn? Order in the file: buildings, lamps, puddles, mountains, vehicles, player, missions, rain, police. PMREM after the sky and city, e.g., right before "/* ----------------------------- vehicles" section. That captures sky + city. Good. But renderer.render inside will use the current camera? PMREM handles its own cameras.

Risk: PMREMGenerator.fromScene with a scene containing sprites/points could be slow but OK.

Let's try it and measure appearance; if it looks bad, remove.

Actually with environment reflections on asphalt (metalness .22, roughness .48) we get a nice wet sheen. Let's do it.

